window.FINAL_MOCK = {"rows":[{"id":"childhood","questions":13,"topics":[{"label":"Childhood Mental Health","id":"week6-childhood","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{}},{"label":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","id":"week6-neurodev","sets":["mustKnow","eaq","medications"],"skip":{"mustKnow":[8],"eaq":[2],"medications":[0,1,8]}},{"label":"Impulse Control Disorders","id":"week6-impulse","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"neurocognitive","questions":9,"topics":[{"label":"Neurocognitive Disorders","id":"week7-neurocognitive","sets":["mustKnow","eaq","medications"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"somatic","questions":8,"topics":[{"label":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","id":"week6-somatic","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"medical","questions":6,"topics":[{"label":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","id":"week7-medical","sets":["mustKnow"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"trauma","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","id":"week5-trauma","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{"mustKnow":[8],"eaq":[6,8,12]}}]},{"id":"anxiety","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","id":"week5-anxiety","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{"mustKnow":[8,9],"eaq":[9,10,11,16,21]}}]},{"id":"schizophrenia","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","id":"week4-schizophrenia","sets":["mustKnow","eaq","medications"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"sud","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","id":"week3-sud","sets":["mustKnow","eaq","medications"],"skip":{}}]},{"id":"bipolar","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","id":"week2-bipolar","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{"mustKnow":[2,3,4,5],"eaq":[1,2,4,8,10,11,13,17,19]}}]},{"id":"depressive","questions":5,"topics":[{"label":"Depressive Disorders","id":"week2-depressive","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{"mustKnow":[2,3,4,5,6],"eaq":[6,8,9,10,25,26,27]}}]},{"id":"anger","questions":4,"topics":[{"label":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","id":"week1-anger","sets":["mustKnow","eaq"],"skip":{}}]}],"clicker":[{"stem":"A 17-year-old adolescent is newly admitted to the unit. While preparing to conduct an admission assessment, the health care team member notices that the adolescent’s parents are in the room and that they are arguing with the adolescent. Which action should the team member take?","options":["Ask them to stop arguing so the parents and the adolescent can be interviewed at the same time.","Ask politely to interview the adolescent and the parents in separate rooms.","Allow them privacy to handle their conflict and postpone the admission assessment until they are finished.","Ask the parents and adolescent with a look of disapproval, “can’t we all just get along?”"],"answer":1,"rationale":"By asking to interview the parents and adolescent in separate rooms, the team member is allowing the adolescent and parents an opportunity to express themselves without provoking an argument; this technique also enhances a therapeutic alliance and prevents a power struggle. Interviewing them together would violate the adolescent’s right to privacy and minimize the chance of building a therapeutic alliance. The admission assessment should be a priority, and by postponing it, the team member would allow the power struggle between the parents and adolescent to persist, which may worsen behavioral issues. A disapproving look is disrespectful to the adolescent and parents, and prompting them to “just get along” is an ineffective attempt at therapeutic communication. The team member should establish a therapeutic alliance with the adolescent and the parents.","topic":"Childhood Mental Health","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which technique can the health care team member use to change the environment and reduce the potential for behavioral issues in an adolescent?","options":["Maintain a clutter-free and clean room for the adolescent.","Make sure the room is very warm so that the adolescent does not get a chill.","Place the adolescent in a room next to a patient who is experiencing mania and who talks nonstop so that they can keep each other company.","Insist that the brightest lights remain turned on at all times so that the adolescent can see the surroundings."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Maintaining physical structures and fixtures and limiting clutter enhance a therapeutic environment. Extreme temperatures, intrusive peers, noise, and lighting are all environmental factors in a clinical setting that may trigger behavioral issues in adolescents.","topic":"Childhood Mental Health","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse on an in-patient pediatric unit is admitting a client diagnosed with an autistic disorder. Which would the nurse expect to assess?","options":["A strong connection with siblings.","An active imagination.","Abnormalities in physical appearance.","Language deficits."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Deficits in social interactions, relationships and language.<br>Stereotypical repetitive speech and/or behaviors<br>Obsessive focus on specific objects (fixed interest)<br>Over adherence to routines or rituals<br>Hyper- or hypo-reactivity to sensory input<br>Extreme resistance to change<br>Fascination with revolving objects (revolving ceiling fan, revolving door)","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A child diagnosed with an autistic disorder makes no eye contact, is unresponsive to staff members, and continuously twists, spins, and head bangs. Which problem would take <b>priority</b>?","options":["Personal identity disorder r/t poor ego differentiation.","Impaired verbal communication r/t withdrawal into self.","Risk for injury r/t head banging.","Impaired social interaction r/t delay in accomplishing developmental tasks."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The headbanging must be stopped due to a risk for injury<br>Safety is always the priority!","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A client diagnosed with Tourette's disorder has a nursing diagnosis of social isolation. Which charting entry documents a successful outcome related to this client’s problem?","options":["Adherent with instructions to use the bathroom before bedtime.","Made craft project during group activity therapy session.","Responding to staff requests to follow unit rules.","Able to focus on tv cartoons for 30 minutes."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Made craft project during group activity therapy session.<br>This entry indicates that the client engaged in a social activity and participated in a group setting, which is a positive sign that they are overcoming social isolation. It reflects active participation, which is an important aspect of socializing and interacting with others in a therapeutic setting.<br>The other options do not directly address the issue of social isolation: adherent with instructions to use the bathroom before bedtime: this is a behavioral outcome but does not address social interaction. Responding to staff requests to follow unit rules: this reflects compliance but doesn't speak to the social interaction or overcoming isolation. Able to focus on TV cartoons for 30 minutes: this shows the client’s ability to focus but does not directly address the client’s social participation or interaction with others.","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A non-stimulant medication for ADHD that also treats related symptoms of anxiety and requires the nurse to educate the parent about the warning signs of suicide is?","options":["clonidine","amiodarone","atomoxetine","methylphenidate"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Atomoxetine (Strattera) is a non-stimulant medication used to treat ADHD and can also help alleviate related symptoms of anxiety. It requires the nurse to educate the parent about the warning signs of suicide, as it carries a <b>black box warning</b> for increased risk of suicidal thoughts and behaviors in children and adolescents, particularly during the early stages of treatment or when the dose is adjusted.<br>The other medications are not the best fit for this scenario:<br><b>Clonidine</b> is sometimes used for ADHD, particularly for hyperactivity and impulsivity, but it does not typically treat anxiety in the same way.<br><b>Amiodarone</b> is a medication used for heart arrhythmias and is unrelated to ADHD treatment.<br><b>Methylphenidate</b> is a stimulant medication used to treat ADHD but does not carry the same suicide risk warning and is not classified as a non-stimulant.","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following are first-line pharmacological treatments for ADHD in children and adolescents?","options":["Clonidine and guanfacine","Methylphenidate and amphetamines","Atomoxetine and lithium","Antipsychotics and mood stabilizers"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Psychostimulants, specifically methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta) and amphetamines (Adderall, Vyvanse), are first-line pharmacological treatments for ADHD. They work by blocking the reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine, with amphetamines also increasing neurotransmitter release.","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A nurse is educating parents about stimulant medications prescribed for their child's ADHD. Which statements should the nurse include in the teaching? (<b>Select all that apply</b>)","options":["These medications work by blocking the reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine","Psychostimulants are controlled substances with potential for misuse","Common side effects include sedation and decreased heart rate","Growth suppression is a possible side effect that should be monitored","The medication can be stopped abruptly without concern","Blood pressure and heart rate should be monitored regularly"],"answers":[0,1,3,5],"rationale":"<b>A. Correct.</b> Psychostimulants are sympathomimetic amines that act by blocking the reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine. Amphetamines also increase neurotransmitter release.<br><b>B. Correct.</b> Psychostimulants are controlled substances with the potential for misuse, which is an important safety consideration for families.<br><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Psychostimulants cause insomnia (not sedation) and increased blood pressure and heart rate (not decreased) due to their norepinephrine- and dopamine-enhancing effects.<br><b>D. Correct.</b> Growth suppression is a possible side effect of psychostimulants and should be monitored throughout treatment. Other side effects include agitation and insomnia.<br><b>E. Incorrect.</b> This statement applies to alpha-2 agonists like clonidine and guanfacine, which should not be discontinued abruptly due to risk of rebound hypertension. While not specifically stated for stimulants in the materials, abrupt discontinuation is generally not recommended.<br><b>F. Correct.</b> Due to the norepinephrine- and dopamine-enhancing effects, psychostimulants increase blood pressure and heart rate, requiring regular monitoring throughout therapy.","topic":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A child diagnosed with a conduct disorder is disruptive and non-adherent with rules in the milieu. Which outcome, related to this client’s problem, should the nurse expect the client to achieve?","options":["The child will maintain anxiety at a reasonable level by day 2.","The child will interact with others in a socially appropriate manner by day 2.","The child will accept direction without becoming defensive by discharge.","The child will contract not to harm self during this shift."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The child will accept direction without becoming defensive by discharge.<br>This goal is realistic because individuals with conduct disorder often exhibit difficulties with authority figures and social rules. However, with time, structured interventions, and support, they can learn to respond to directions in a more controlled and non-defensive way. It is also a longer-term goal that allows for progress over time rather than an immediate expectation.<br>The other options are less realistic for a child with conduct disorder:<br><b>Maintaining anxiety at a reasonable level</b> by day 2 may not directly address the core behavior issues associated with conduct disorder.<br><b>Interacting in a socially appropriate manner</b> by day 2 might be too ambitious given the nature of conduct disorder, which involves significant behavioral and social difficulties.<br><b>Contracting not to harm self</b> during this shift is more specific to suicidal ideation or self-harm concerns and may not be the central issue for a child with conduct disorder unless there are other factors present.","topic":"Impulse Control Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A child diagnosed with oppositional defiant disorder is cursing then yells loudly at staff when asked to leave group therapy. Which nursing intervention would be appropriate?","options":["Administer prn medication to decrease acting-out behavior.","Accompany the child to a quiet area to decrease external stimuli.","Institute seclusion following agency protocol.","Allow the child to stay in group therapy and monitor the situation further."],"answer":1,"rationale":"<b>Accompany the child to a quiet area to decrease external stimuli.</b><br>Children with oppositional defiant disorder (ODD) often exhibit defiant, argumentative, and disruptive behaviors. By removing the child from the stimulating environment (such as a group therapy setting), you provide an opportunity for the child to calm down in a less intense space. This allows the staff to de-escalate the situation and address the behavior in a more controlled manner, avoiding further escalation.<br>The other options are less appropriate:<br><b>Administering prn medication</b> may not be the first intervention and should only be considered if the behavior becomes unmanageable or if the child’s agitation is severe enough to warrant pharmacological intervention, but this is usually not the first line of action.<br><b>Instituting seclusion</b> would be an extreme measure and should only be considered if the child poses an imminent risk to themselves or others, which doesn't seem to be the case here.<br><b>Allowing the child to stay in group therapy</b> could escalate the behavior further, child has already demonstrated disruptive behavior and may need a break to regain composure before returning to the group setting.","topic":"Impulse Control Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"An 86-year-old man is experiencing hyperkinetic delirium. Which of the following nursing interventions would the nurse initiate?","options":["Medicate with amitriptyline IM; set limits to decrease agitation; provide all care at once to limit stimulation.","Restrain patient to prevent self-injury; medicate with Haldol IM; re-establish trust with the patient.","Stop all medications suspected of contributing to delirium; hydrate; put the bed in lowest position.","Begin combination medication therapy with memantine and donepezil; obtain an order for constant observation."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The priority intervention is to stop all medications that could be potentially contributing to the delirium, hydrate the patient and provide safety by lowering the bed. Restraint is avoided, a lower dose of Haldol should be tried before giving a full 10mg; memantine and donepezil are used for dementia and are not acute care medications. Amitriptyline is not indicated and setting limits would not be effective for a patient experiencing delirium.","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"On a 24-hour assessment, the nurse documents that a client diagnosed with Alzheimer’s disease presents with aphasia. Which client behavior supports this finding?","options":["The client is sad and has no ability to experience pleasure.","The client is extremely emaciated and appears to be wasting away.","The client is having difficulty forming words.","The client is having difficulty swallowing."],"answer":2,"rationale":"C: Aphasia is the term used when an individual is having difficulty communicating through speech, writing, or signs. This is often caused by dysfunction of brain centers. Aphasia is a cardinal symptom observed in Alzheimer’s disease.<br>A is anhedonia<br>B is Cachexia<br>D is dysphagia","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"You are caring for a client diagnosed with Neurocognitive disorder due to Alzheimer’s disease who was admitted 72 hours ago. The client states, “Last night I went on a wonderful dinner cruise.” This is which type of communication, and what is the underlying reason for its use?","options":["The client is using confabulation to achieve secondary gains.","The client is using confabulation to protect the ego.","The client is using perseveration to divert attention.","The client is using perseveration to maintain self-esteem."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The client is using confabulation. Confabulation is the creation of imaginary events to fill in memory gaps. The underlying reason is to protect the ego by maintaining self-esteem, not to achieve secondary gains. Perseveration is repeating the same words or idea in response to different questions. This does not increase self-esteem.","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A client presents in the emergency department with an acute decline in cognitive ability. The nurse’s assessment should include which of the following? <b>Select all that apply</b>.","options":["Family history and a mini-mental status examination","Laboratory tests and vital signs","Toxicology screen for illegal substances.","Open-ended questions to obtain information.","Familiarizing the client with the milieu.","Educate the family on the etiology of cognitive decline"],"answers":[0,1,2],"rationale":"Should include family history, MMSE, VS, blood and urine samples, hepatic and renal function, blood glucose, electrolytes, toxic substances, pain, O2 sats, etc.<br>Close-ended, not open-ended questions can be effective in focusing a client with an acute decrease in cognition.<br>Familiarizing the client with the milieu and educating the family are important, but these are interventions and not an assessment","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A hospitalized patient diagnosed with delirium misinterprets reality. A patient diagnosed with dementia wanders about the home. Which outcome is the priority in both scenarios? The patients will","options":["Remain safe in the environment.","Participate actively in self-care.","Communicate verbally.","Acknowledge reality."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Risk for injury is the nurse’s priority concern. Safety maintenance is the desired outcome. The other outcomes are lower priorities and may not be realistic.","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Goals of care for an older adult patient diagnosed with delirium caused by fever and dehydration will focus on","options":["demonstrating motor responses to noxious stimuli.","identifying stressors negatively affecting self.","exerting control over responses to perceptual distortions.","returning to premorbid levels of function."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The desired overall goal is that the delirious patient will return to the level of functioning held before the development of delirium. Demonstrating motor response to noxious stimuli is an indicator appropriate for a patient whose arousal is compromised. Identifying stressors that negatively affect the self is too nonspecific to be useful for a patient with delirium. Exerting control over responses to perceptual distortions is an unrealistic indicator for a patient with sensorium problems related to delirium.","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A nurse is interviewing a client with the diagnosis of dementia of the Alzheimer type. What question should the nurse ask to assess the client’s orientation to place?","options":["“Where are you?”","“Who brought you here?”","“Do you know where you are?”","“How long have you been here?”"],"answer":0,"rationale":"\"Where are you?\" is the best question with which to elicit information about the client’s orientation to place, because it encourages a response that can be assessed. Asking who brought the client focuses on recent memory; it does not assess orientation to place. \"Do you know where you are?\" can be answered yes or no and will not objectively reveal the client’s orientation. \"How long have you been here?\" focuses on orientation to time, not place.","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which behavior indicates a patient is experiencing illness anxiety?","options":["Reporting chest pain to avoid certain unpleasant activities","Fainting and vomiting to seek attention from significant others","Using a walker unnecessarily to acquire financial gain from a disability plan","Asking for blood pressure to be measured frequently because of dizziness and nausea"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Asking for frequent blood pressure measurements indicates illness anxiety. The patient needs frequent reassurance. Avoiding certain unpleasant activities and acquiring financial gain are examples of malingering, not illness anxiety. Seeking attention from significant others is not illness anxiety.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse is caring for an adult patient with somatic symptom disorder who reports frequent headaches and stomachaches. Although the physical assessment did not reveal any gastrointestinal issues, the patient is convinced the healthcare provider is wrong. Which is the best response by the nurse?","options":["\"The stomachache is not real; it is all in your head.\"","\"I can call the healthcare provider in here to reassess your stomach.\"","\"I understand that your stomach may be hurting; it must be frustrating to have that sensation.\"","\"For right now, try to imagine that your stomach does not hurt so that you can finish eating your breakfast.\""],"answer":2,"rationale":"Patients with somatic symptom disorder may report physical symptoms, such as stomachaches, and may believe that there is something wrong with them, even if physical assessments prove otherwise. Although it can be frustrating for the nurse, the nurse must always believe that the patient’s symptoms are real and treat the patient as such. Therefore, empathizing with the patient is the best response by the nurse. It is not appropriate or therapeutic to tell the patient that the symptoms are not real. It is not necessary to call the healthcare provider for another assessment. It is inappropriate to push the patient to eat when they are experiencing pain.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A nurse is assessing a patient who reports body pain. The patient seems to be anxious about the presence of a serious medical illness. Which disorder would the nurse suspect for the patient?","options":["Factitious disorder","Conversion disorder","Illness anxiety disorder","Somatic symptom disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Illness anxiety disorder is seen when the patient obsessively misinterprets that a physical sensation is the result of a serious medical illness. In the case of a factitious disorder, the person pretends to be ill to seek attention and get emotional needs met. Conversion disorder is seen with neurological symptoms when a neurological diagnosis is absent. In somatic symptom disorder, physical findings or medical diagnosis may be absent, but distressing symptoms and excessive response are present.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A woman suddenly finds she cannot see. She seems unconcerned about her symptom and tells her spouse, \"Don't worry, dear. Things will all work out.\" Which term reflects her attitude?","options":["Regression","Depersonalization","La belle indifference","Dissociative amnesia"],"answer":2,"rationale":"La belle indifference is an attitude of unconcern about a symptom that is unconsciously used to lower anxiety. Regression is an anxiety defense mechanism. Depersonalization and dissociative amnesia are dissociative disorders.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient diagnosed with a somatic disorder asks what kind of treatment will be prescribed. Which response would the nurse provide?","options":["\"A combination of antianxiety and antidepressant therapy is the most effective therapy.\"","\"Aversion therapy often is used because, in effect, you are punishing yourself by not being able to walk.\"","\"As you see desired behaviors modeled by the therapist, you also will be able to achieve the expected outcome.\"","\"Cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) has been shown to consistently provide the best outcome for these types of disorders.\""],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy is the most consistently supported treatment for the full spectrum of somatic disorders. A combination of antianxiety and antidepressant therapy, aversion therapy, and emulating desired behaviors are incorrect and do not describe the most used and effective therapy for this disorder.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which factor would the nurse keep in mind when working with a patient who has conversion disorder?","options":["The patient may show neurological symptoms.","The patient displays these symptoms to gain attention or get out of work or school.","The patient displays a great deal of anxiety about the loss of neurological function.","The disorder may progress to psychosis, including command hallucinations."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Patients with conversion disorder show neurological symptoms in the absence of an organic cause. Malingering is behavior the patient displays to gain attention or to get out of work or school. The patient with a conversion disorder may display \"la belle indifference\" or an unusually calm reaction to the loss of a neurological function. Conversion disorder does not progress to psychosis.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which assessment data would alert the nurse to the possibility of malingering in a patient who is first admitted with reports abdominal pain, dizziness, and headaches and later reports back pain?","options":["The patient was adopted at the age of 5 years.","The patient was raised primarily by a single parent.","The patient has a court date this week for drunk driving.","The patient has a history of oppositional-defiant disorder."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Malingering is a process of fabricating an illness or exaggerating symptoms to gain a desired benefit or avoid something undesirable, such as to obtain prescription medications, evade military service, or evade legal action. It is more common in men, those who have been neglected or abused in childhood, and those who have had frequent childhood hospitalizations. Adoption is not known to be a causative factor in malingering. Being raised in a single parent home or by a primarily female influence is not known to be a causative factor in malingering. A history of oppositional-defiant disorder is not known to a causative factor in malingering.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient suddenly develops paralysis in the bilateral lower extremities. The neurologist finds no neurological disorder. Which disorder would the nurse suspect for the patient?","options":["Factitious disorder","Conversion disorder","Illness anxiety disorder","Somatic symptom disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The patient has paralysis of the lower limbs. This deficit in the voluntary motor function is a neurological symptom. There is, however, no neurological diagnosis to explain the neurological symptom. <b>The nurse therefore suspects the patient of having conversion disorder.</b> Factitious disorder is a deliberate act of pretending to be ill. Patients with such disorders consciously fabricate symptoms or cause self-inflicted injuries to be treated as a \"patient.\" Illness anxiety disorder is obsessive thought about illness. Such patients interpret even normal bodily changes as signs and symptoms of serious illnesses. In somatic symptom disorder, the patient's suffering is real. The patient actually experiences a high level of functional impairment and may report chest pain, shortness of breath, back pain, insomnia, and abdominal pain. Biological causes of these symptoms are identified only in a few patients.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"When working with clients with somatic symptom disorders, nurses should extensively explore symptoms from other doctor visits during each visit.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Nurses should not explore symptoms in this case. For example, they avoid statements such as, “Why did you go to see Dr. Jones the pain specialist?” or “Tell me what you discussed in your visit to the other primary care provider.”<br>KEY take away point: When you are verbally interacting with this patient, do <b>NOT</b> focus on the symptom or complaint, <b>DO</b> focus on the patient’s feelings.","topic":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","source":"Week 6 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A medical mimic is a disorder that presents with medical symptoms directly resulting from an underlying medical condition.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Medical mimics are disorders that present with <b>psychiatric</b> symptoms directly resulting from an underlying medical condition. The cause of the psychiatric symptoms is the medical illness.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"High risk individuals for an underlying medical mimic include which of the following (<b>Select all that apply</b>)","options":["Clients from a high socioeconomic strata","Clients with preexisting medical illness","Clients with a history of substance abuse","Clients that are elderly","Clients with an unusual response to treatment"],"answers":[1,2,3,4],"rationale":"Groups considered to be at higher risk for medical illness presenting with psychiatric symptoms include the elderly, patients with a history of substance abuse, patients without a prior history of psychiatric illness, patients with preexisting medical illness, and patients from <b>lower</b> socioeconomic strata, and those with an unusual response to treatment (worse after receiving an antipsychotic or anxiolytic).","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following patient symptoms would a nurse rule out as a psychiatric side effect of pharmacologic treatment:","options":["Severe confusion after taking diphenhydramine, an over-the-counter antihistamine","Increased anxiety after discontinuing benzodiazepine","Decreased manic symptoms resulting from lithium carbonate dose adjustment","Suicidal thoughts after starting fluoxetine"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The clinical presentation of psychiatric side effects (PSEs) often resembles spontaneous psychiatric syndromes. These can occur at usual doses, in cases of intoxication, or during the days following withdrawal of a given treatment. PSEs range from short-lasting anxiety to severe confusion, and alleged cases of suicide have been reported. A decrease in manic symptoms is expected when lithium doses are adjusted.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The triage nurse’s documentation can influence the caregivers in the department through their initial assessment and entries in the electronic record. Which of the following entries is most likely to lead to a misdiagnosis?","options":["\"25-year-old female, ‘feeling anxious and can’t breathe,’ history of panic attack\"","\"25-year-old female, short of breath, tightness in chest, two weeks postpartum.\"","\"25-year-old female, short of breath, tightness in chest, hr 90, temp 97.8, bp 142/95\"","“25-year-old female reports sudden onset shortness of breath and chest tightness. Appears mildly distressed. No known cardiac or pulmonary history”"],"answer":0,"rationale":"It is important to remain neutral and factual while charting to minimize cognitive bias. In the first answer, \"25-year-old female, ‘feeling anxious and can’t breathe,’ history of panic attack\" one is dealing with a possible anxiety attack, while the wording of the other examples demonstrate clinical suspicion for pulmonary embolism. Unintentional labeling of patients in triage can raise or lower the clinical antennae of physicians and nursing staff, and thus negatively impact the patient’s care. The above example also highlights another potential assumption that serves to limit the extent that other medical causes are explored. Caregivers may assume that a patient with a history of prior psychiatric diagnosis, treatment, or admissions most likely is presenting with an exacerbation of the same condition.<br>Documentation that reduces misdiagnosis:<br>Uses <b>objective data</b><br>Avoids diagnostic labels in triage<br>Includes relevant risk factors<br>Encourages continued assessment rather than implying a conclusion","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A client is scheduled for hip replacement surgery. The client says to the nurse, “I am so afraid that it will hurt and will make me worse off than I am.” Which of the following is the best statement to the client?","options":["“Can you tell me what you understand about the procedure?”","“Your fears are a sign that you really should have this procedure.”","“Try not to worry. This is a well-known and easy procedure for the health care provider.”","“Those are very normal fears, but please be assured that everything will be okay.”"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Medical conditions are stressful and life changing, and the emotional dimensions of these conditions must be addressed. The correct option is a therapeutic communication technique that explores the client’s feelings, determines the level of client understanding about the procedure, and displays caring. Option 2 demeans the client and does not encourage further sharing by the client. Option 3 diminishes the client’s feelings by directing attention away from the client and to the health care provider’s importance. Option 4 does not address the client’s fears and puts the client’s feelings on hold.<br>Test-Taking Strategy: Note the strategic word, <b>best</b>. Use therapeutic communication techniques and remember to focus on the client’s feelings and concerns. Option 1 is the only option that addresses the client’s feelings, encourages client verbalization, and displays caring.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A 25-year-old female presents to the emergency department with shortness of breath and chest tightness. She has a history of anxiety and panic attacks. Which of the following triage questions is most appropriate to help avoid misdiagnosis?","options":["“Is this similar to your previous panic attacks?”","“Are you feeling especially stressed today?”","“Are these symptoms different in any way from your previous anxiety episodes?”","“Did you take your anxiety medication today?”"],"answer":2,"rationale":"This question promotes critical thinking and reduces <b>anchoring bias</b> and <b>premature closure</b>. It encourages comparison to prior episodes while keeping the differential diagnosis open. Patients with anxiety can still develop serious medical conditions such as pulmonary embolism, asthma exacerbation, arrhythmia, or acute coronary syndrome.<br><b>Option A</b> may reinforce the assumption that the symptoms are anxiety-related.<br><b>Option B</b> narrows the focus to psychological causes.<br><b>Option D</b> assumes the diagnosis is anxiety before ruling out medical causes.<br>By asking whether the symptoms are different, the nurse gathers important diagnostic information without prematurely labeling the complaint.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What should a nurse recognize when assessing the behavior of a medical or surgical client?","options":["No connection exists between physical and behavioral problems.","Mental illness has nothing to do with physical illness.","Changes in physical status can affect behavior.","Physically ill patients always display physical signs of illness."],"answer":2,"rationale":"When a client has a physical illness, physical status can cause behavioral changes; thus, behavioral reactions are considered part of the illness. Behavioral changes can occur whether the client has a mental health diagnosis. Physical and behavioral problems are intertwined, not separate entities. Physically ill clients may not always display physical signs of illness; sometimes, physical illness can be confirmed only via diagnostic tests.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A client experiencing significant stress associated with a disturbing new medical diagnosis asks the nurse, “Do you think saying a prayer would help?” Select the nurse’s best answer.","options":["“It could be that prayer is your only hope.”","“You may find prayer gives comfort and lowers your stress.”","“We do not have evidence that prayer helps, but it wouldn’t hurt.”","“I can help you feel calmer by teaching you meditation exercises.”"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Many clients find that spiritual measures, including prayer, are helpful in mediating stress. Studies have shown that spiritual practices can enhance the sense of well-being. When a client suggests a viable means of reducing stress, it should be supported by the nurse. Indicating that prayer is the client’s only hope is pessimistic and would cause further distress. Suggesting meditation or other alternatives to prayer implies that the nurse does not think prayer would be effective.","topic":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","source":"Week 7 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"In which scenario would a diagnosis of Acute Stress Disorder most likely be appropriate?","options":["A soldier experiences nightmares about combat six months after returning home.","A person is unable to recall the details of a car accident that happened two weeks ago.","An individual develops persistent avoidance of reminders of a trauma three months after the event.","A survivor of a natural disaster feels detached from reality one year later."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A person is unable to recall the details of a car accident that happened two weeks ago. ASD symptoms, including dissociative amnesia, can occur shortly after a traumatic event (within 3 days to 1 month) while the other scenarios describe symptoms occurring beyond that timeframe.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following best describes the concept of “dissociation” in trauma-related disorders?","options":["Complete loss of memory for past events","Emotional detachment from the present moment","Repeatedly reliving traumatic events through flashbacks","Persistent negative mood and feelings of guilt"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dissociation refers to a disconnection between thoughts, identity, consciousness, and memory, often leading to emotional detachment from the present moment. It can manifest as feeling disconnected from one’s body or surroundings and is a common symptom in trauma-related disorders like PTSD. Complete memory loss (A) is not typical in most trauma-related disorders, although memory gaps may occur in some cases. Flashbacks (C) are related to intrusive recollections, not dissociation. Persistent negative mood (D) is a common symptom of PTSD but not specifically dissociation.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a common symptom of PTSD?","options":["Hypervigilance","Flashbacks","Memory loss for events unrelated to the trauma","Avoidance of reminders of the trauma"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Memory loss for events unrelated to the trauma is not a typical symptom of PTSD. While PTSD can involve dissociative amnesia, this typically affects memories related to the traumatic event, not unrelated events. General memory loss is more indicative of other cognitive disorders. The other options are common symptoms that occur in PTSD.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following is a primary difference between Acute Stress Disorder (ASD) and Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)?","options":["ASD is only diagnosed in children, while PTSD is for adults.","PTSD involves symptoms that persist for more than one month, whereas ASD symptoms last less than one month.","ASD symptoms are more severe than those of PTSD.","PTSD only occurs following natural disasters, while ASD can result from any traumatic event."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The main difference between Acute Stress Disorder (ASD) and Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) is the duration of symptoms. ASD lasts for three days to four weeks after a traumatic event, while PTSD symptoms persist for more than one month. Both disorders can occur in adults and children, and both can result from any traumatic event, not just natural disasters.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"<b>Case study.</b> Max, age 6, has been traumatized by a history of violence from a father with alcohol use disorder. A neighbor calls the police one night, and the father is taken in for questioning and agrees to enroll in rehab. Max is taken to the hospital for assessment of his arm, which is covered with bruises. His mother is distraught and says she wants help for the whole family, including her husband. The nurse prepares to assess Max’s mental health.<br><br>Before going home from the hospital, Max and his family begin intervention therapy. Which is probably the least useful strategy at this time?","options":["Establish trust and safety in the therapeutic relationship","Use art to help Max express his feelings.","Bring Max’s father into 1:1 play therapy sessions","Teach Max and his parents coping skills."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Providing safety and stabilization is the first stage in intervention. This is done by creating a safe predictable environment; establishing trust and safety in the therapeutic relationship; stopping self-destructive behaviors; providing education about trauma and its effects. Play therapy is conducted by a trained professional and does not include the perpetrator of abuse. Art and play therapy assist in reducing arousal, symptom reduction which is stage 2. Stage 3 involves problem solving, social skills training, and coping skills training.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following should be established first in working with a child with recent trauma?","options":["Provide safety and stabilization","Reduce symptoms","Develop a value system","Provide support for catching up on developmental and social skills"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Stage 1 involves providing safety and stabilization. Only when the child feels safe can treatment concentrate on things like symptom reduction, catching up on skills, and developing a value system.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following is the most prevalent type of child abuse?","options":["Physical Abuse","Sexual Abuse","Verbal Abuse","Neglect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Neglect is the most common form of child abuse reported across the globe, including in countries like the United States. Neglect involves failure to provide for a child’s basic physical, emotional, educational, or medical needs, including: inadequate supervision; failure to provide food, shelter, or clothing; lack of access to necessary medical care; &amp; ignoring emotional needs","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A 10-year-old child was placed in a foster home after being removed from parental contact because of abuse. The child has apprehension, tremulousness, and impaired concentration. The foster parent also reports the child has an upset stomach, urinates frequently, and does not understand what has happened. What helpful measures should the nurse suggest to the foster parents? The nurse should recommend (Select All That Apply)","options":["Conveying empathy and acknowledging the child’s distress.","Explaining and reinforcing reality to avoid distortions.","Using a calm manner and low, comforting voice.","Avoiding repetition in what is said to the child.","Staying with the child until the anxiety decreases.","Minimizing opportunities for exercise and play."],"answers":[0,1,2,4],"rationale":"The child’s symptoms and behavior suggest that he is exhibiting PTSD. Interventions appropriate for this level of anxiety include using a calm, reassuring tone, acknowledging the child’s distress, repeating content as needed when there is impaired cognitive processing and memory, providing opportunities for comforting and normalizing play and physical activities, correcting any distortion of reality, and staying with the child to increase his sense of security.","topic":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"You are ready to give your first insulin injection to a patient and are worried about drawing up the insulin correctly. Your instructor asks you a question. You do not answer, so the instructor speaks louder and repeats the question. You look up, apologize, and state, “I didn’t hear what you said.” Which of the following levels of anxiety does this represent?","options":["Mild","Moderate","Severe","Panic"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Moderate: This is selective inattention, and the perceptual field narrows. As anxiety increases, the perceptual field narrows, and some details are excluded from observation. The person experiencing moderate anxiety sees, hears, and grasps less information and may demonstrate selective inattention, where only certain things in the environment are seen or heard unless they are pointed out. The ability to think clearly is hampered, but learning and problem solving can still take place, though not at an optimal level. Sympathetic nervous system symptoms begin to kick in at this level. The individual may experience tension, a pounding heart, increased pulse and respiratory rates, perspiration, and mild somatic symptoms (e.g., gastric discomfort, headache, urinary urgency). Voice tremors and shaking may be noticed. Mild or moderate anxiety levels can be constructive because anxiety may be a signal that something in the person’s life needs attention or is dangerous. (Halter p. 268)","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What behaviors might be exhibited that would indicate that your patient is experiencing panic-level anxiety?","options":["Seeing and grasping information quickly to make problem solving more effective","Greatly reduced and distorted perceptual field, confusion and sense of impending doom","Completely unable to process what is happening, hallucinations and shortness of breath","Voice tremors, poor concentration and headache"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Panic is the most extreme level of anxiety and results in markedly dysregulated behavior. Someone in a state of panic is unable to process what is going on in the environment and may lose touch with reality. The behavior that results may be manifested as pacing, running, shouting, screaming, or withdrawal. Hallucinations, which are false sensory perceptions, such as seeing something that is not there or hearing voices, may be experienced. Physical behavior may become erratic, uncoordinated, and impulsive. Automatic behaviors are used to reduce and relieve anxiety, although such efforts may be ineffective. Acute panic may lead to exhaustion. (Halter p. 269)","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A hallmark of several anxiety disorders, including generalized anxiety disorder, phobias, agoraphobia, and obsessive-compulsive disorder, is that individuals with these disorders:","options":["Believe they are “normal” in every way and that they do not need treatment","Do not present with visible objective signs of distress","Choose to engage in disorder behaviors to get out of social obligations","Recognize that their symptoms are out of proportion to any real threat"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Most individuals with anxiety disorders can identify one or more factors that bring on their anxiety responses. Most of these individuals recognize that their feelings of anxiety are far out of proportion to any real dangers, but they are unable to control the anxiety they feel.","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Patient 1: Emily<br>Symptoms: Avoids social gatherings and public speaking due to intense fear of being judged or embarrassed. Experiences sweating, trembling, and heart racing when faced with social interactions.<br>Based on her symptoms, what is the most appropriate diagnosis for Emily?","options":["Panic Disorder","Generalized Anxiety Disorder","Social Anxiety Disorder","Specific Phobia"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Emily’s fear of social situations, along with physical symptoms of anxiety when she anticipates or engages in these situations, aligns with Social Anxiety Disorder. This disorder is characterized by a marked fear of social or performance situations where the individual is exposed to scrutiny.","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Patient 2: Sarah<br>Symptoms: Excessive worry about her job performance, health, and family safety, difficulty sleeping and muscle tension, reports feeling restless and irritable.<br>Based on her symptoms, what diagnosis is most appropriate for Sarah?","options":["Panic Disorder","Generalized Anxiety Disorder","Social Anxiety Disorder","Acute Stress Disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Sarah exhibits excessive worry about various aspects of her life, which is a hallmark of GAD. The symptoms of restlessness, irritability, and muscle tension further support this diagnosis. GAD is characterized by persistent, excessive anxiety that is difficult to control and is present more days than not for at least six months.","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Patient 3: Lisa<br>Symptoms: Experiences intense fear and panic when in enclosed spaces, such as elevators or crowded rooms. She avoids situations where she might feel trapped, leading to significant disruptions in her daily activities.<br>Based on her symptoms, what is the most appropriate diagnosis for Lisa?","options":["Generalized Anxiety Disorder","Social Anxiety Disorder","Obsessive Compulsive Disorder","Specific Phobia"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Lisa’s intense fear of enclosed spaces, accompanied by panic symptoms and avoidance behavior, indicates a Specific Phobia. Claustrophobia is a common type of specific phobia that involves fear of being in situations where escape may be difficult.","topic":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","source":"Week 5 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A nursing instructor is teaching her students about the etiology of schizophrenia. What statement by the nursing student indicates an understanding of the content presented?","options":["“Schizophrenia is a disorder of the brain that can be cured with the correct treatment.”","“A person inherits schizophrenia from a parent.”","“Problems in the structure of the brain cause schizophrenia.”","“There are lots of potential causes for this disease and continues to be a controversial topic.”"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The etiology of schizophrenia remains unclear. No single theory or hypothesis has been postulated that substantiates a clear-cut explanation of this disease. The more research that is conducted, the more evidence is compiled to support the concept of multiple causes in the development of schizophrenia. The most current theory seems to be that schizophrenia is a biologically based disease with a genetic component. The onset of the disease also is influenced by factors in the internal and external environments.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse assesses a patient diagnosed with schizophrenia. Which assessment finding would the nurse regard as a negative symptom of schizophrenia?","options":["Anosognosia","Flight of ideas","Avolition","Motor agitation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Negative symptoms include avolition, affective blunting, apathy, alogia, anhedonia and asociality. Anosognosia is a cognitive symptom. Motor agitation and flight of ideas are both positive symptoms.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following is a positive symptom of schizophrenia?","options":["Affective blunting","Anhedonia","Hallucinations","Asociality"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Hallucinations are a positive symptom of schizophrenia. Anhedonia and asociality or social withdrawal are negative symptoms of schizophrenia. Affective blunting is an affective symptom.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia struggles with problem-solving and decision-making tasks, such as deciding on a daily schedule or handling finances. What cognitive symptom is this?","options":["Impaired memory","Impaired executive functioning","Attention deficits","Disorganized behavior"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Impaired executive functioning affects the ability to plan, solve problems, and make decisions, such as managing a daily schedule or finances. Impaired memory involves difficulty with short-term information, attention deficits involve problems with focus, and disorganized behavior includes unusual or erratic actions.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient on an in-patient psychiatric unit refuses to take medication because, “The pill has a special code written on it that makes it poisonous!” What kind of delusion is the patient likely experiencing?","options":["Nihilistic","Grandiose","Persecutory","Somatic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A persecutory delusion is a type of delusion in which the patient believes he/she is being conspired against, cheated, spied on, followed, poisoned or drugged, harassed, or obstructed in the pursuit of long-term goals; a nihilistic delusion is a belief that the mind, body, or the world at large—or parts thereof—no longer exists; a grandiose delusion is a type of delusion in which the individual has an irrational idea regarding self-worth, talent, knowledge, or power; a somatic delusion is a type of delusion in which the individual believe they have some sort of physical defect, disorder, or disease.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia is exhibiting signs of paranoia and is reluctant to take their medication due to fears of being poisoned. What should the nurse’s initial approach be?","options":["Reassure the patient that the medication is safe and encourage them to take it","Confront the patient’s fears directly and insist they take the medication","Explore the patient’s fears and involve them in decision-making about their treatment","Withhold the medication until the patient’s fears are resolved"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Exploring the patient’s fears and involving them in treatment decisions can help build trust and address their concerns. It is important to understand and validate their feelings while working collaboratively to alleviate their fears. Reassuring them without addressing their concerns might be ineffective and confronting them or withholding medication could damage the therapeutic relationship.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"During a nursing assessment, a patient with schizophrenia expresses paranoia about the staff. What is the best initial response for the nurse?","options":["“You don’t need to worry about the staff; they are here to help you.”","“I understand that you feel this way, but we need to focus on your treatment.”","“It’s important to trust the staff; they have your best interests in mind.”","“Can you explain more about your feeling that the staff might be against you?”"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Asking the patient to elaborate on their feelings allows the nurse to better understand their perspective and can help in building trust. It is important to acknowledge and explore the patient’s feelings without dismissing or invalidating their experiences. This approach helps in developing a therapeutic relationship and addressing the paranoia constructively.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia reports that they are hearing voices telling them to harm themselves. What is the nurse’s most immediate action?","options":["Assess patient’s ability to recognize that the voices are not real","Ask the patient what the voice is telling them to do","Distract the patient with engaging activities","Avoid discussing the voices to prevent agitation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"This is an example of a command hallucination. This type of hallucination must be monitored carefully because they may be dangerous. It is essential to assess first, what the patient hears; what it is telling them to do? Secondary to that we would assess the patient’s ability to recognize the hallucination as “not real” and the patient’s ability to resist commands.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia is experiencing severe agitation and is at risk for harming themselves or others. What is the priority intervention?","options":["Administer antipsychotic medication","Use de-escalation techniques","Ensure the patient’s environment is safe","Encourage the patient to talk about their feelings"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Ensuring a safe environment is the priority when a patient is at risk for self-harm or harm to others. This includes removing any potential hazards and closely monitoring the patient. While medication, de-escalation techniques, and talking about feelings are important, safety is the immediate concern in this situation.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia is exhibiting signs of disorganized speech, such as incoherent and fragmented responses. What is a suitable nursing intervention to facilitate communication?","options":["Speak very slowly and use complex sentences to ensure understanding","Ask direct and simple questions, and provide clear and concise responses","Avoid engaging in conversation to prevent further disorganization","Encourage the patient to write down their thoughts to improve coherence"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Asking direct and simple questions and providing clear, concise responses helps facilitate communication with a patient who has disorganized speech. Using complex sentences or avoiding conversation may not improve understanding, and writing down thoughts might not be practical for all patients.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse is assessing a patient diagnosed with schizophrenia. The patient states, “We want to take the bus, but the airport took all the traffic.” Which charting entry accurately documents this symptom?","options":["The patient is experiencing loose associations.","The patient is attempting to communicate by using word salad.","The patient is experiencing delusional thinking.","The patient is experiencing an illusion involving planes."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Loose associations is thinking characterized by speech in which ideas shift from one unrelated subject to another. The client is unaware that the topics are unconnected; Word salad is a group of words that are strung together in a random fashion without any logical connection; Delusions are false beliefs; Illusions are misperceptions or misinterpretations of real external stimuli.","topic":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","source":"Week 4 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A 26-year-old patient with a history of heroin abuse states, “I’ve been using more heroin lately because I have begun to need more to feel the effect I want.” What does this statement describe?","options":["Intoxication","Withdrawal","Addiction","Tolerance"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Tolerance is described as needing increasingly greater amounts of a substance to receive the desired result to become intoxicated or finding that using the same amount over time results in a much-diminished effect. Intoxication is the effect of the drug. Withdrawal is a set of symptoms patients experience when they stop taking the drug. Addiction is loss of behavioral control with craving and inability to abstain, loss of emotional regulation, and loss of the ability to identify problematic behaviors and relationships.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Dan, who has been admitted to the alcohol rehabilitation unit after being fired for drinking on the job, states to the nurse, “I don’t have a problem with alcohol. I can handle my booze better than anyone I know. My boss is just a jerk! I haven’t missed any more days than my coworkers.” What is the nurse’s best response?","options":["“Maybe your boss is mistaken, Dan.”","“You are here because your drinking was interfering with your work, Dan.”","“Get real, Dan! You’re an alcoholic and you know it!”","“Why do you think your boss is a jerk, Dan?”"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The nurse is using the therapeutic communication technique of restating or paraphrasing. She is direct but nonjudgmental (states the facts without shaming). This helps the patient connect consequences to behavior which is also used in motivational interviewing. This approach aligns with therapeutic communication principles and supports the stages of change model, where individuals with substance use issues may still be in denial (precontemplation stage). The other options would not be therapeutic including blame and stigmatizing language and asking “why” questions. A. Invalidates reality and reinforces denial rather than guiding Dan toward insight. C. Confrontational and shaming, likely to cause resistance or shutdown. D. Focuses on blaming others rather than addressing Dan’s own behavior and its consequences.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Dan, who has been admitted to the alcohol rehabilitation unit after being fired for drinking on the job, states to the nurse, “I don’t have a problem with alcohol. I can handle my booze better than anyone I know.” Which defense mechanism is Dan using?","options":["Denial","Compensation","Displacement","Rationalization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Denial is an attempt to screen or ignore unacceptable realities by refusing to acknowledge them. Compensation is covering up weaknesses by emphasizing a more desirable trait or by overachievement in a more comfortable area. Displacement is the transferring or discharging of emotional reactions from one object or person to another object or person. Rationalization is justification for behaviors by faulty logic and ascribing motives that are socially acceptable but did not in fact inspire the behavior.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Dan begins attending AA meetings following discharge. Which of the statements by Dan reflects the purpose of this organization?","options":["“They claim they will help me stay sober.”","“I’ll dry out in AA, then I can have a social drink now and then.”","“AA is only for people who have reached rock bottom.”","“If I lose my job, AA will help me find another one.”"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The primary purpose of AA is to help individuals achieve and maintain sobriety from alcohol through peer support, fellowship, and the 12-step program. This statement reflects an accurate understanding of what AA offers—support for staying sober, not necessarily treatment, job assistance, or allowing controlled drinking. B: Misunderstands the purpose of AA. AA promotes complete abstinence, not controlled or social drinking. C. Inaccurate. AA is open to anyone who wants to stop drinking, regardless of how severe their situation is. Early intervention is welcome. D. AA is a support group, not a job placement service. It may offer emotional support, but not direct employment assistance.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which of the following concepts would the nurse use to explain the disease of addiction to the patient? Someone who has an addiction to heroin:","options":["May really want to cut down but is unable to control the addiction.","Has a habit that can be changed if they have enough willpower.","Will always have a damaged brain that will never heal.","Always has a root cause for the addiction related to abuse in their past."],"answer":0,"rationale":"This statement accurately reflects the chronic, relapsing nature of addiction as a brain disease. People with substance use disorders often desire to stop or cut down but experience impaired control over their use due to neurobiological changes in the brain. This aligns with the medical model of addiction, which recognizes that addiction affects the brain’s reward, motivation, and self-control systems—making quitting extremely difficult without support or treatment. B. Oversimplifies addiction and promotes stigma. Addiction is not just a habit; it’s a complex medical condition that requires more than willpower. C. Too absolute and pessimistic. While addiction does alter brain function, the brain can heal and adapt over time, especially with sustained recovery. D. Not always true. While past trauma or abuse can be a risk factor, addiction can develop without such history. There are multiple contributing factors (genetic, psychological, environmental).","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient is being prepared for discharge. The patient states, “Dr. Jones is putting me on some medication called naltrexone. How will that help me?” Which response by the nurse is appropriate teaching regarding naltrexone?","options":["“It will help your mood so that you do not feel the need to use drugs.”","“It is a sedative that will help you sleep at night, so you are more alert and able to make good decisions.”","“It will keep you from experiencing withdrawal symptoms.”","“It helps to prevent relapse by reducing drug cravings.”"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Naltrexone is used for withdrawal and to prevent relapse by reducing the craving for the drug. None of the other options accurately describe the action of naltrexone. Remember: Naltrexone may be used to treat opioid addiction or dependence or alcohol addiction. Do not confuse naltrexone with naloxone (Narcan), which strips the opioid from the opioid receptor and is used for the emergency treatment of opioid overdose.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse is discussing Wernicke’s encephalopathy with another healthcare provider. Which of the following statements by the nurse are accurate statements about WE? (SELECT ALL THAT APPLY)","options":["WE is NOT reversible","WE is caused by chronic alcoholism","WE is caused by chronic opioid use","WE may cause delirium/psychosis","WE is reversible with thiamine (vitamin B1) replacement"],"answers":[1,4],"rationale":"WE is caused by chronic alcohol use due to the decrease in intestinal absorption of thiamine (vitamin b1) by 70%. WE is reversible with thiamine (vitamin b1) replacement. Delirium/psychosis occur in Korsakoff Syndrome. Mild to severe mental confusion occurs in WE.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse is correct when they state that SBIRT is: (SELECT ALL THAT APPLY)","options":["“The delivery of early intervention and treatment services for persons with SUD in general and those at risk.”","“The goal of SBIRT is to reduce and prevent related health consequences, disease, accidents, and injuries, reduce costs and healthcare utilization.”","“The delivery of early intervention and treatment services for persons with SUD related to alcohol and those at risk.”","“The AUDIT tool is used for adolescents and the CRAFFT tool is used for adults.”","“The AUDIT tool is used for adults and the CRAFFT tool is used for adolescents.”"],"answers":[0,1,4],"rationale":"SBIRT is the delivery of early intervention and treatment services for persons with SUD in general and those who are at risk. It can be used to screen for use and severity of alcohol, illicit drugs, and prescription drug use, misuse, or abuse. The AUDIT tool is used for adults and the CRAFFT tool is used for adolescents; the goal of SBIRT is to reduce and prevent related health consequences, disease, accidents, and injuries, reduce costs and healthcare utilization. C is incorrect because it is not limited to just alcohol. D is flipped.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What are the signs and symptoms associated with opioid withdrawal?","options":["Anxiety, hallucinations and gooseflesh skin.","Lacrimation, rhinorrhea, dilated pupils, and bone aches.","Hallucinations, disorientation, tachycardia, and tremors.","Agitation, headache, and tremors."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Opioid withdrawal symptoms: resting pulse rate, sweating, restlessness, pupil size, bone or joint aches, runny nose or tearing, GI upset, tremor, yawning, anxiety or irritability, gooseflesh skin. Scores: 5-12: mild; 13-24: moderate; 25-36: moderately severe; >36: severe.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What are the signs and symptoms associated with sedative hypnotic/alcohol withdrawal?","options":["Anxiety, hallucinations and gooseflesh skin","Lacrimation, rhinorrhea, dilated pupils, and bone aches","Hallucinations, disorientation, tachycardia, and tremors","Agitation, headache, and tremors"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Sedative hypnotic/alcohol withdrawal: CIWA. Nausea/vomiting, tremors, anxiety, agitation, paroxysmal sweats, orientation, tactile disturbance, auditory disturbance, visual disturbance, headache. Scores: 0-9: minimal withdrawal; 10-19: mild-moderate; >20: severe.","topic":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","source":"Week 3 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A client is hospitalized in an acute manic phase. During the first few days of caring for this client, the nurse’s primary plan of care needs to include a focus on the client’s degree of:","options":["Exhaustion and nutrition","Decreased self-esteem and inappropriate grooming","Flirtations and provocative behavior","Demanding and impulsive behavior"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Remember that mania is a medical emergency. Not all clients have demanding and impulsive behavior and the other options are not as urgent.","topic":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which action should be included in the initial plan of care for the first 24 hours for a client exhibiting symptoms of mania?","options":["Assist the client in establishing a quiet environment.","Place the client in seclusion for his safety.","Ensure that the therapeutic lithium level is reached","Challenge or confront delusions or hallucinations."],"answer":0,"rationale":"[The deck gives the answer on its key slide with no rationale slide; this is what the instructor said about it in class.] We definitely want to get them in a quiet environment and get them to slow down. We would not put them in seclusion — there is nothing in the stem saying they need it, and a patient is only locked in a room if they are trying to hurt themselves or someone else. We would want to look at lithium levels and monitor them, but that is not what the first 24 hours focus on. And we would never challenge delusions or hallucinations.","topic":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What activity is best for a client in manic phase?","options":["Activity like volleyball engaging large muscle groups","Walking with the nurse","Quiet activity like bingo","Solitary activity like a puzzle"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Walking with a staff member who can provide supervision and redirection as necessary would be most therapeutic and allows minimal energy exertion. Puzzles may be too complicated and frustrating for a client with mania. Bingo is not a quiet activity and would be overstimulating. Volleyball would have too much energy expenditure.","topic":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The primary goal of treatment during the acute manic phase of bipolar disorder is to:","options":["Complete the history and physical","Sleep 6 hours for two consecutive nights","Stabilize mood and prevent harm","Get the patient to shower"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The primary goal during the acute manic phase is to stabilize the patient’s mood and ensure their safety, as manic episodes can involve impulsive behavior, agitation, and a risk of harm to self or others.","topic":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The nurse’s initial approach in caring for a client with major depression with psychomotor retardation, reporting low energy to even get dressed, would be to:","options":["Encourage the client to select own meals.","Involve the client in group therapy.","Sit silently at the client’s side when client does not feel like talking.","Provide cheerful activities"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Initially the patient is extremely slow so we would sit with them silently to show that we are there for them and to establish therapeutic rapport. With psychomotor retardation they would not be able to pick their own meals, participate in groups, or in cheerful activities.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which physical aspect will be most important for the nurse to assess in a client with depression?","options":["Sleep patterns","Bladder function","Bowel function","Socialization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Sleep pattern disturbances are a sign of depression. Bowel and bladder function are not a significant clinical sign in the depressive disorders. Socialization behaviors are not a physical aspect.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which question would be a priority for the nurse when assessing for symptoms of major depression in a client?","options":["“Will you tell me about any special powers you believe you have?”","“You look really sad. Have you ever thought of ending your life?”","“Your family says you never stop. How much sleep do you get?”","“Do you ever find that you don’t remember where you’ve been or what you’ve done?”"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Safety first. The materials emphasize that “risk for suicide is increased when depression is accompanied by hopelessness, substance use problems, a recent loss or separation, a history of past suicide attempts, and acute suicidal ideation.” Always assess suicide risk before addressing other symptoms.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A nurse is assessing a 9-year-old child who has been frequently irritable and has severe temper outbursts three or more times per week in multiple settings (home and school). These symptoms have persisted for over a year. Which of the following diagnoses is most appropriate to consider?","options":["Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD)","Bipolar Disorder","Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD)","Intermittent Explosive Disorder (IED)"],"answer":2,"rationale":"DMDD is characterized by severe, recurrent temper outbursts and persistent irritability in children aged 6–18 in at least 2 settings. Oppositional Defiant Disorder involves defiant and hostile behavior but lacks the severe, persistent irritability and frequent temper outbursts across settings seen in DMDD. Bipolar Disorder includes episodic mood changes, while DMDD features chronic, non-episodic irritability. Intermittent Explosive Disorder typically appears in older individuals (age 18+), and outbursts are impulsive rather than chronically mood-based as in DMDD.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"<b>Case study.</b> Jeff, 19, is brought to the hospital after a suicide attempt. His parents found him in his back yard, wearing his favorite jeans and t-shirt, and with one of his father’s neckties. He had overdosed on some of his “mother’s pills”, and his stomach was pumped in the ED with return of many pill fragments. He has just been admitted to your floor for observation.<br><br>During intake, Jeff doesn’t speak much and shows no emotion. His parents stated that they have observed weight loss and decreased appetite, insomnia, fatigue, worthlessness or guilt, loss of interest in his college classes and even the online games he usually plays with friends, and “constant sadness.”<br><br>Which assessment findings are most concerning and require immediate follow-up by the nurse? <i>Select all that apply.</i>","options":["Jeff reports difficulty sleeping","Jeff has lost interest in online games","Jeff expresses feelings of worthlessness","Jeff is emotionally flat and minimally communicative"],"answers":[2,3],"rationale":"A flat affect and minimal communication following a suicide attempt may indicate ongoing suicidal intent. Sudden emotional calm or withdrawal can be a red flag for imminent risk. [The rest of this rationale was written for this page: the deck asked this as a single-answer question keyed to the flat affect alone and argues only that option, but the instructor said in class she intended it as a select-all.] Worthlessness is equally concerning — it is one of the feelings that drives a suicide attempt, and voicing it this soon after one signals the intent has not resolved. Difficulty sleeping and loss of interest are both real symptoms of depression, but neither is the immediate safety priority.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Jeff’s parents have described his lack of interest in things he used to enjoy, like games with his friends, and his classes, which he used to like. This may be best described by the term","options":["Inappetence","Impedance","Indolence","Anhedonia"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Anhedonia refers to (an “without” + hedone “pleasure” = inability to feel happy). Inappetence — loss or lack of appetite. Impedance — something that impedes or is a hindrance. Indolence — laziness or idleness.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which statement made by Jeff would indicate the greatest improvement in his condition?","options":["“I don’t really feel like talking right now.”","“I still feel tired most of the time.”","“I’m glad my parents found me, and I want help.”","“College has been overwhelming lately.”"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Expressing relief about surviving and willingness to accept help suggests decreased suicidal intent and increased insight, which are key indicators of improvement.","topic":"Depressive Disorders","source":"Week 2 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"The patient is restrained. As his nurse, what is your priority?","options":["Debrief the patient","Ensure the patient’s safety","Administer a sedating medication","Obtain an order from the health care provider"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Once in restraint, a patient must be directly observed and formally assessed at frequent, regular intervals for level of awareness, level of activity, safety within the restraints, hydration, toileting needs, nutrition, and comfort.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which patient behavior is a criterion for mechanical restraint?","options":["Screaming profanities","Assaulting a staff person","Refusing a medication dose","Throwing a pillow at another patient"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Indications for the use of mechanical restraint include protecting the patient from self-harm and preventing the patient from assaulting others.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"What are some predictors of violence? (Select all that apply)","options":["History of violence","Active listening","Recent acts of violence","Using profanity","Hyperactivity"],"answers":[0,2,3,4],"rationale":"Predictors of violence include: recent acts of violence, using profanity, hyperactivity, history of violence.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"Which is an effective nursing intervention/action to assist an angry patient to learn to manage anger without violence?","options":["Help the patient identify a thought that produces anger, evaluate the validity of the belief, and substitute reality-based thinking.","Provide negative reinforcement such as restraint or seclusion in response to angry outbursts, whether violence is present.","Use aversive conditioning, such as popping a rubber band on the wrist, to help extinguish angry feelings.","Administer medication."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Anger has a strong cognitive component, so using cognition techniques to manage anger is logical. The incorrect options do nothing to help the patient learn anger management.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient was arrested for breaking house windows of a former domestic partner. The patient’s history also reveals childhood abuse by a punitive parent, torturing family pets, and an arrest for disorderly conduct. Which is the nurse’s primary concern?","options":["Risk for injury","Ineffective coping","Impaired social interactions","Risk for violence toward others"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Defining characteristics for risk for other-directed violence include a history of being abused as a child, having committed other violent acts, and demonstrating poor impulse control. There is no indicator that the patient will experience injury. Ineffective coping and impaired social interaction have lower priorities.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"},{"stem":"A patient is pacing the hall near the nurses’ station, swearing loudly. An appropriate initial intervention for the nurse would be to address the patient by name and say:","options":["“Why are you pacing?”","“Please be quiet and sit down in this chair immediately.”","“I’d like to talk with you about how you’re feeling right now.”","“You must go to your room and try to get control of yourself.”"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Intervention should begin with analysis of the patient and the situation. When anger is escalating, a patient’s ability to process decreases. It is important to speak to the patient slowly and in short sentences, using a low and calm voice. Use open-ended statements designed to hear the patient’s feelings and concerns. This leads to the next step of planning an intervention.","topic":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","source":"Week 1 Lecture Review"}],"extra":[{"stem":"Oxidative stress, wear-and-tear, and neuroendocrine are examples of which theory of aging?","options":["Genetic","Biological","Psychosocial","Developmental"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Theories about the process of aging are classified as biological, developmental, and psychosocial. Oxidative stress, wear-and-tear, and neuroendocrine are examples of biological theories of aging. Genetic is not a category in the major theories of aging. Psychosocial theories of aging include role, activity, disengagement, continuity, age-stratification, and modernization. Developmental theories of aging include Jung’s theory of personality, Erikson, Peck, Maslow, and Tornstam. (p. 571)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which statement regarding Jung’s theory of personality development is correct?","options":["Disengagement from the world can be a time of introspection leading to wisdom.","Cells accumulate damage, resulting in errors of replication in the cellular processes.","Individuals move from outward achievement to self-acceptance during development.","Self-actualization and the evolution of developmental needs occur as the individual ages."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The theories of aging help the nurse guide the patient for better living. Development in an individual is explained by Jung’s theory of personality, which states that individuals move from outward achievement to self-acceptance. Tornstam’s theory states that disengagement from the world can be a time of introspection leading to wisdom. Cells accumulating damage resulting in errors of replication is cell functioning theory. It explains the biological changes caused by aging. Maslow’s theory states that self-actualization and the evolution of developmental needs occur as the individual ages. (p. 571)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which category of aging theory includes the theory that mutual withdrawal occurs between the aging person and others?","options":["Immunity","Biological","Psychosocial","Developmental"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Theories about the process of aging are biological, developmental, or psychosocial. Disengagement is a psychosocial theory of aging. It relates to a mutual withdrawal that occurs between the aging person and others. Immunity is a type of biological theory of aging. Developmental theories on aging include Jung’s theory of personality, as well as theories from Erikson, Peck, Maslow, and Tornstam. (p. 571)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which information about the older adult population is accurate?","options":["Older adults are unable to learn new tasks.","Most older adults are socially isolated and lonely.","Older widows appear to adjust better than younger ones.","Older adults are more rigid in their thinking and set in their ways."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The various myths about aging influence the psychological condition of older adults. Society’s perception reduces the morale of older adult patients. Research suggests that older widows appear to adjust better than younger ones. The patient tries to cope with the situation and adjusts according to the present situation. That older adults are socially isolated and lonely is a myth. They are not isolated, but some older adult patients may be neglected by their children who make them feel lonely, and they get depressed. That older adults are unable to learn new tasks is a myth. A person with interest and zeal to learn can perform any task. Patients must be encouraged to perform tasks that enhance confidence. It is a myth that older adults are more rigid in their thinking and set in their ways. (p. 571)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which patient score indicates severe pain according to the Pain Assessment in Advanced Dementia (PAINAD) scale?","options":["1","3","5","7"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The PAINAD scale uses five categories to assess pain, which include breathing, negative vocalization, facial expression, body language, and consolability. This is used by healthcare providers to determine if a patient needs treatment. A score of 7 to 10 indicates severe pain. A score of 1 to 3 indicates mild pain. A score of 4 to 6 indicates moderate pain. (p. 568)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which major theory of aging is from the psychosocial perspective?","options":["Error theory","Maslow’s theory","Tornstam’s theory","Disengagement theory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Disengagement theory comes from the psychosocial perspective and suggests that mutual withdrawal occurs between the aging person and others. Error theory is biological, stating that error in protein synthesis results in impaired cellular function. Tornstam’s theory is a developmental theory that posits disengagement with the world can be a time of introspection leading to wisdom. Maslow’s theory is from the developmental perspective in which self-actualization and the evolution of developmental needs occur as the individual ages. (p. 571)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"},{"stem":"Which factor is part of the assessment of the higher-level cognitive domain of a patient?","options":["Attention","Problem solving","Orientation to the environment","Recognition of previously acquired information"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The higher-level cognitive domain includes the ability to problem solve. Attention, orientation to the environment, and recognition of previously acquired information occur in the lower-level domain. (p. 431)","topic":"Neurocognitive Disorders","source":"eaq"}]};
